    "result_text": "All 3 acceptance tests pass and the build succeeds.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented address normalization applied at owner creation, following the codebase's existing normalizer pattern (`TelephoneNormalizer`/`HouseholdNormalizer`).\n\n**New files:**\n- `util/AddressNormalizer.java` \u2014 a `@Component` whose `normalize(String)` trims, collapses whitespace, upper-cases, and expands abbreviations token-by-token (`ST\u2192STREET`, `RD\u2192ROAD`, `AVE\u2192AVENUE`). It throws `InvalidAddressException` when the value is blank after normalization.\n- `rest/advice/InvalidAddressException.java` \u2014 a runtime exception for the blank-address case.\n\n**Modified files:**\n- `rest/advice/ExceptionControllerAdvice.java` \u2014 added a handler mapping `InvalidAddressException` to **400 Bad Request** (mirroring the telephone handler).\n- `rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java` \u2014 injected `AddressNormalizer` and normalize the address at the **top** of `normalizeOwnerFields(...)`, which runs before the household duplicate check and the shared-`householdId` derivation. Because both read `owner.getAddress()` after this point, every address comparison now uses the normalized form, and the normalized value is what gets stored and returned.\n\n**On the required-field check:** the generated DTO already rejects whitespace-only input via its `(?s).*\\S.*` pattern, so `\"   \"` is caught at bean-validation time; the normalizer's own blank check is the enforcing rule for the normalized value (and a defensive backstop), satisfying the spec's \"reject an address that is blank after normalization.\"\n\nNotes:\n- I left `updateOwner` unchanged, since the spec scopes normalization to owner **creation**.\n- Existing tests verified: `\"  12  main  st \"` \u2192 `\"12 MAIN STREET\"`, `\"7 elm ave\"` \u2192 `\"7 ELM AVENUE\"`, and `\"   \"` \u2192 400.",
